  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Tuesday: Forecast remains on track for today for \nelevated to locally critical fire weather conditions for the \nOkanogan valley despite being cooler today. Boundary layer winds\nare much weaker than yesterday but with slope alignment again, \nthere is 20-40% chance of briefly meeting red flag warning \ncriteria this afternoon and early evening for the Okanogan \nvalley. Dewpoints west of US-395 are 5-15 degrees drier than \nyesterday as drier air has filtered into central WA being \nbrushed by a decaying cold front, so relative humidities will \ndip into the 10-20% range this afternoon. Further east in \neastern WA and north ID, conditions are cool thanks to cloud \ncover from monsoonal moisture. The airmass moisture content in \nplace hardly changes into tomorrow and Tuesday, but temperatures\nwill sharply rise into the 80s to low 100s as height rises take\nplace. HeatRisk will increase from minor today to moderate and \nlocally major by Tuesday, affecting folks without proper \ncooling/hydration. Similar elevated to locally critical fire \nweather conditions are expected for the Okanogan valley Monday \nand Tuesday. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: Ensembles are into good agreement \nthat a trough will be off the coast Wednesday, but the evolution\nof it still remains unclear. Ensembles are split essentially \n50/50 with this trough coming onshore or staying offshore by \nThursday. Unsurprisingly, having the low offshore would keep \nstronger ridging over the area leading to temperatures in the \nlow 90s to low 100s with a 10-30% chance of drier thunderstorms.\nIf the low were to come ashore, temperatures would be much \ncooler in the 70s and 80s with greater chances (20-40%) of \nslower, wetter thunderstorms. With any threat of thunderstorms \nwill come the threat of fire starts and gusty outflow winds. \nForecast confidence actually is higher beyond midweek for above \naverage temperatures in the 90s to low 100s to return (or \npersist depending on how mid-week plays out) as high pressure in\nthe northern plains retrogrades west back into the \nintermountain west. /DB",
